 \begin{abstract}
 \renewcommand{\baselinestretch}{1.5}\selectfont
-Graph-free node classification predicts from node attributes when graph relations are unavailable or costly at deployment. Existing noise-robust graph methods retain graph-dependent inference, whereas graph-free methods largely assume clean labels. We propose Granular Ball Local-Consensus Soft Supervision (GB-LCSS) for transductive node classification with noisy labels and graph-free inference. GB-LCSS learns label-free graph contrastive representations, freezes them, and recursively groups nodes into granular balls. For each training node, labels of the other training nodes in its ball form a leave-one-out consensus whose agreement determines how strongly it is blended with the observed label to form a soft target for a multilayer perceptron (MLP). This separates region construction from noisy-label aggregation and reduces the influence of isolated annotation errors. Across five datasets and five noise settings, GB-LCSS achieves the highest five-seed mean accuracy in 24 of 25 settings among seven noisy-label baselines sharing the same deployed MLP. Its soft targets also have lower mean Brier scores than the observed one-hot labels in all 25 settings.
